(i.e. g'' does not decay to a much smaller magnitude within these
windows -- it stays comparable to, or grows past, A -- confirming
the quadratic lower bound g(t*)-g(t) >= (A_low/2)(t-t*)^2 holds with
A_low ~ 0.189*A throughout, not just infinitesimally near t*).

=== (2) Resulting doubly-exponential tail bound (Gaussian-type) ===
With A_low = f*A (f the ratio found above), the standard Gaussian
tail estimate gives, for the truncated region |t-t*|>K/sqrt(A):
  integral_{|s|>K} e^{-f s^2/2} ds  <=  (2/(K sqrt(f))) * e^{-f K^2/2}
  K=8: tail bound (relative to central Gaussian mass sqrt(2pi)) ~ 1.341e-03
  K=12: tail bound (relative to central Gaussian mass sqrt(2pi)) ~ 4.587e-07
  K=20: tail bound (relative to central Gaussian mass sqrt(2pi)) ~ 8.170e-18
These bounds are independent of n (they depend only on K and the
worst-case curvature ratio f found in (1)), so they hold uniformly
across the whole tested (lambda,gamma) grid at K=12: already <1e-6,
utterly negligible next to the O(n^{-1/2})/O(n^{-1}) orders tracked
in scripts 03/04 at any n large enough for those orders to be
meaningful. Part (3) below measures the ACTUAL tail directly by
quadrature and finds it far smaller still than even this bound.

